# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 3 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 has "once_nothing → ¬something_began" and "¬something_began → now_nothing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 has "once_nothing → ¬something_began" and "once_nothing ∧ ¬something_began → now_nothing"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 has "once_nothing → ¬something_began"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "at one time there was nothing in existence", "something began to exist after a time when nothing was in existence" and "even now there is nothing in existence" are false. Is S5, "Now if this were true, even now there would be nothing in existence, because that which does not exist only begins to exist by something already existing.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 have "¬now_nothing"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 has "once_nothing ∧ ¬something_began → now_nothing" and "¬now_nothing"; they differ when only "at one time there was nothing in existence" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "at one time there was nothing in existence" is true and "something began to exist after a time when nothing was in existence" and "even now there is nothing in existence" are false. Is S6, "Therefore, if at one time nothing was in existence, it would have been impossible for anything to have begun to exist; and thus even now nothing would be in existence--which is absurd.", true then?
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 has "each_once_not → once_nothing" and "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "only_caused_necessity → infinite_regress"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 have "¬all_contingent → necessary_exists" and "only_caused_necessity → infinite_regress"; they differ when only "everything is possible not to be", "each thing, being possible not to be, at some time is not", "there exists something the existence of which is necessary", "every necessary thing has its necessity caused by another" and "there is an infinite series of necessary things having their necessity caused by another" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 takes for granted "if each thing, being possible not to be, at some time is not, then at one time there was nothing in existence"; aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.2 and aquinas-third-way.logic.r3.3 do not
aquinas-third-way.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-third-way.logic.r3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if each thing, being possible not to be, at some time is not, then at one time there was nothing in existence"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/negation: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 P3 (S3): the sentence says "impossible", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if everything is possible not to be, then each thing, being possible not to be, at some time is not"
aquinas-third-way.md:1: argue/unfaithful: aquinas-third-way.logic.r3 P9 (S11): the sentence has no word of "there exists some being having of itself its own necessity, not receiving it from another": is it the sentence P9 came from?
